<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Southern Rhodesia</h1><p class="st-meta">1923 CE – 1965 CE · capital Salisbury</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The self-governing British settler colony that became Rhodesia and, after a long war, Zimbabwe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In a 1922 referendum the settlers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company's</a> southern territory rejected union with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">South Africa</a>. On 1 October 1923 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> annexed it as the self-governing Colony of Southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rhodesia">Rhodesia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-coghlan">Charles Coghlan</a> as its first premier and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harare">Salisbury</a> as its capital, ending <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/company-rule-in-rhodesia">Company rule in Rhodesia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A small white electorate controlled parliament, while <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> kept rarely used reserve powers over African affairs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-apportionment-act">Land Apportionment Act</a> of 1930 divided land by race, giving the best farmland to whites. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-huggins">Godfrey Huggins</a>, prime minister from 1933 to 1953, led the colony through rapid growth based on tobacco, mining and white immigration after 1945, while African nationalism grew through the 1950s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rhodesia">Rhodesia</a> led the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a> from 1953 to 1963. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodesian-front">Rhodesian Front</a> won power in 1962, and on 11 November 1965 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ian-smith">Ian Smith's</a> government declared independence unilaterally, creating Rhodesia.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/southern-rhodesia.jpg" alt="Southern Rhodesia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Greentubing · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Southern_Rhodesia_(1924%E2%80%931964).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1923 CE – 1965 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Salisbury</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Charles Coghlan, Godfrey Huggins, Ian Smith</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Shona, isiNdebele</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony of Southern Rhodesia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=southern-rhodesia" aria-label="Southern Rhodesia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5492 2882 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Southern Rhodesia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5812 3138L4997 651M5812 3138L5842 3096M5812 3138L5842 3096M5812 3138L5770 3192M5812 3138L5842 3096"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M5842 3096h0M5842 3096h0M5770 3192h0M5842 3096h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5812 3138h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5812 3138h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=southern-rhodesia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
